From 3b13c23a2247293c293e3a9ed884cc066548405d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 08:09:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09390/11362] Unwedge milestone .558: prior_failures for
 exp6481

Second conductor wedge in eight hours, same guard. exp6481 scope-matched two
priors with no prior_failures block, so the exclusion-manifest linter refused
activation and the conductor looped every two minutes.

exp6437 genuinely never ran: its one gate required exp6436 to report
v554_queue_ready_score == 1.0 and got 0.0, so it was refused at pre-gate.
Nothing about the receipt contract was measured, and this task declares no
dependency on that chain.

exp6449 IS NOT A FAILURE. Its verdict reads "success: path receipt controls
localized and critical attacks failed closed" -- a terminal success reporting
that attacks failed CLOSED, the desired outcome. It is matched as a prior
failure because map_status_label checks failure tokens before win tokens, so
"failed" inside "failed closed" outranks the terminal prefix.

I started to fix that precedence and reverted it. Making the terminal prefix win
outright fails test_mixed_terminal_verdict_still_not_complete, which encodes a
deliberate decision: a verdict like "complete_..._weak_fit_..._hardware_leg_
blocked" must stay Blocked. Measured corpus impact of the change I nearly made:
561 artifacts would have been relabelled, 324 Blocked -> Complete and 39
Failed -> Complete, including "complete: kan shape manifest ready; synthesis and
board claims blocked" -- where the old label is the correct one. That is a
research-record reclassification, not a bug fix, and not mine to make alone.

Worth recording that this shape has happened before. The 2026-07-01 note in
in_process_doc_reconcile.py describes a clean exp5128 success being read as a
prior failure and HARD-blocking exp5140 as a doomed rerun it wasn't. That fix
rescued CLEAN wins by adding success/shipped/passed to the win tokens, and
deliberately left mixed-token verdicts alone. exp6449 is the residual case: a
success whose prose contains a negative word. The accepted remedy is this
block, not new classification semantics.

Verified additive: every field other than prior_failures is byte-identical, and
the linter now reports clean.

